{"id":"homes-bought","title":"What the City paid for the buildings it bought","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"When the City buys a building, how does the price per home compare with its other purchases, and with the value the assessor carried before the sale?","finding":"The Board approved 41 purchases of property inside the city since 2014, $1,045,663,961 in all (including one $326,690,953 deal to have a new City office building built); 9 of them, $370,000,000, were buildings for homeless housing. Where the number of homes is known (9 buildings: 7 of the homeless-housing purchases and 2 other residential buildings) the price runs from $200,000 a home (333-7th Street) to $852,941 (333-12th Street), with a middle value of $268,919. The two official counts of homes at 333-12th Street differ: the State’s Homekey award list gives 170 ($852,941 a home) and the Board’s Budget and Legislative Analyst, in the report on the purchase file and in the 2024 audit, gives 200 ($725,000 a home). The records do not say why the counts differ.","how":"Every passed Board file that buys real property inside the city. Price, seller and address are read from the file title. Homes come from the State’s Homekey award lists where they cover the building (projects are tied to buildings as in the housing-supply screen on Homekey buildings), then the Budget Analyst’s 2024 audit, otherwise the units or rooms on the assessor roll; only finished buildings bought to house people are compared per home (land bought under a new development is listed but not compared). The assessor value is land plus improvements in the last roll on disk (2019 or 2022) from before the purchase.","limits":"A new apartment block and an old residential hotel are different goods, so a high price per home is a reason to read the appraisal, not a finding. Assessed value is capped by Proposition 13 and is often a fraction of market value for a building held for decades; the “times assessed” column is context, not a measure of overpayment. Repair costs after purchase are not in the price. Campaign matches are on a firm’s name only. Official counts of homes differ for 333-12th Street: 170 on the State’s Homekey list, 200 in the Budget Analyst’s audit; the per-home column uses the State’s figure ($725,000 a home on the analyst’s count), and the source of the difference is not known. Official counts of homes differ for 5630-5638 Mission Street: 51 on the State’s Homekey list, 52 in the Budget Analyst’s audit; the per-home column uses the State’s figure ($326,923 a home on the analyst’s count), and the source of the difference is not known. Amounts, parties and dates taken from Board file titles are read by machine: in a hand check of 50 titles, 11 had at least one field read wrongly (4 after later fixes).","columns":[{"k":"building","t":"Building","type":"text"},{"k":"seller","t":"Seller","type":"org"},{"k":"year","t":"Year","type":"year"},{"k":"purpose","t":"Bought for","type":"text"},{"k":"price","t":"Purchase price","type":"usd"},{"k":"homes","t":"Homes or rooms","type":"int"},{"k":"perHome","t":"Price per home","type":"usd"},{"k":"perSqFt","t":"Price per sq ft of building","type":"usd"},{"k":"timesMedian","t":"Against the middle purchase","type":"times"},{"k":"assessed","t":"Assessed value before","type":"usd"},{"k":"timesAssessed","t":"Price against assessed","type":"times"},{"k":"lastSale","t":"Last changed hands","type":"date"},{"k":"note","t":"What else the file shows","type":"text"},{"k":"politics","t":"Seller’s political money","type":"text"},{"k":"file","t":"Board file","type":"link"}],"rows":[{"building":"333-12th Street","seller":"City Gardens Bridge, LLC","sellerKey":null,"date":"2022-04-05","year":"2022","purpose":"Homeless housing","price":145000000,"total":147540000,"homes":170,"homesBasis":"State Homekey award list (Budget Analyst audit: 200)","homesAudit":200,"perHome":852941,"assessed":21018800,"rollYear":"2019","timesAssessed":null,"stale":true,"lastSale":"2018-04-11","heldYears":4.0,"area":21630,"perSqFt":null,"note":"Homes: the State’s Homekey Round 2 award list gives 170 units for City Gardens (168 assisted); the Budget Analyst’s 2024 audit lists 200. The per-home price uses the State’s 170. The 2019 roll shows the site before the building the City bought was finished.","politics":"","politicsTotal":0.0,"sponsors":"Matt Haney, Hillary Ronen, Ahsha Safai, Gordon Mar","file":{"t":"File 220344","u":"https://sfgov.legistar.com/LegislationDetail.aspx?ID=5548588&GUID=DE5470F4-50A9-4ACA-A5C3-155A1295B0BC"},"timesMedian":3.2},{"building":"42 Otis Street","seller":"Costanoan LLC","sellerKey":null,"date":"2023-09-05","year":"2023","purpose":"Homeless housing","price":14200000,"total":14240000,"homes":24,"homesBasis":"State Homekey award list","homesAudit":null,"perHome":591667,"assessed":11307760,"rollYear":"2022","timesAssessed":1.3,"stale":false,"lastSale":"2016-07-15","heldYears":7.2,"area":6124,"perSqFt":null,"note":"","politics":"","politicsTotal":0.0,"sponsors":"Hillary Ronen, Shamann Walton, Rafael Mandelman","file":{"t":"File 230928","u":"https://sfgov.legistar.com/LegislationDetail.aspx?ID=6342884&GUID=CF69398F-10A7-4D55-91B8-A546D54F6401"},"timesMedian":2.2},{"building":"1321 Mission Street","seller":"Mission Smartspace Senior LLC","sellerKey":null,"date":"2021-09-14","year":"2021","purpose":"Homeless housing","price":86500000,"total":86673000,"homes":160,"homesBasis":"Budget Analyst audit","homesAudit":160,"perHome":540625,"assessed":64822053,"rollYear":"2019","timesAssessed":1.3,"stale":false,"lastSale":"2011-06-21","heldYears":10.2,"area":14400,"perSqFt":null,"note":"","politics":"","politicsTotal":0.0,"sponsors":"Matt Haney, Ahsha Safai","file":{"t":"File 210967","u":"https://sfgov.legistar.com/LegislationDetail.aspx?ID=5140615&GUID=591516DB-37D2-455E-AE7F-009A02797E75"},"timesMedian":2.0},{"building":"5630-5638 Mission Street","seller":"Jamna Investments, LLC","sellerKey":null,"date":"2021-09-07","year":"2021","purpose":"Home